          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is walking good or bad for them?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance doesn't say whether walking is good or bad for knee osteoarthritis specifically.\n\nThe closest source is \"Exercising with osteoarthritis: what the program contains and how it feels\" (General clinical reference and rheumatology educational summary). It says exercise for osteoarthritis combines three parts: strengthening the muscles around the joint, keeping the joint moving through its range, and aerobic activity for general fitness and body weight. Walking would fall under the aerobic part. The same source says that supervision at the start matters, because the right movements, load and progression are hard to judge alone.\n\nThe other retrieved sources cover diabetes, COPD, anxiety and high blood pressure. The blood pressure summary describes brisk walking as moderate activity, but it says nothing about knees.\n\nA clinician or physiotherapist who knows your knees can tell you what level of walking suits you. If your knee pain changes during or after walking, such as sudden severe pain, the knee giving way, or heavy swelling or heat, contact a clinician promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
