          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What about swimming instead?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance does not say whether swimming is good or bad for knee osteoarthritis specifically.\n\nThe closest source is \"Exercising with osteoarthritis: what the program contains and how it feels\" (General clinical reference and rheumatology educational summary). It describes three parts of exercise for osteoarthritis: strengthening the muscles around the joint, keeping the joint moving, and aerobic activity for fitness and body weight. Swimming could fit into the aerobic part, but this source does not name it. It also says that supervision at the start matters, because the right movements and load are hard to judge alone.\n\nThe blood pressure summary, \"Physical activity for high blood pressure\" (General clinical reference and physical activity guidance), lists swimming as an example of moderate activity. It does not discuss knees.\n\nA clinician or physiotherapist can tell you whether swimming, walking or another activity suits your knees and how much to do. If swimming or any activity causes sudden severe knee pain, the knee giving way, or heavy swelling or heat, contact a clinician promptly.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
